# LeakSanitizer suppressions for G-LSAN on macOS (.port/tools/gates/sanitizers.sh
# --gate lsan; run_crate picks this file when `uname -s` is Darwin, lsan.supp
# elsewhere).
#
# Same rules as lsan.supp: every entry is a THIRD-PARTY allocation (here: Apple
# system libraries), each with a row in .port/phase-2/SANITIZER_SUPPRESSIONS.md
# (S3-S17) giving the reason and evidence, and a WHY comment directly above it
# (D6 rule c). A leak in Rustty code is never suppressed.
#
# Why macOS needs its own file: LeakSanitizer on Darwin takes its roots from
# thread stacks and registers, the writable sections of loaded modules, and
# three VM-tagged regions (compiler-rt lsan_common_mac.cpp:
# ProcessGlobalRegions, ProcessPlatformSpecificAllocations). Per-thread data
# that libobjc and CoreFoundation reach only through pthread thread-specific
# data, and SkyLight state reached from dispatch sources, are not among them,
# so those blocks are reported as leaked while their owners are alive. Apple's
# `leaks` tool, which does see those roots, reports 0 leaks for the same test
# binaries built natively (SANITIZER_SUPPRESSIONS.md, S3-S17). Each pattern below
# names the allocating system function, so an object that Rustty allocates and
# fails to release (its stack runs through our code, or through
# _objc_rootAllocWithZone under our call) is still reported: G-LSAN's first
# macOS run found the Dear ImGui Metal backend's real leaks that way (UD376).

# S3 (SANITIZER_SUPPRESSIONS.md): libobjc's per-thread "classes being
# initialized" list, created by +initialize on each thread that first touches
# a class; owned by the thread's objc TSD.
leak:_fetchInitializingClassList
# S4: libobjc's per-thread @synchronized lock cache (objc_sync_enter's
# fetch_cache), owned by the thread's objc TSD.
leak:fetch_cache
# S5: CoreFoundation's per-thread TSD table (__CFTSDGetTable), created the first
# time a thread calls into CF; freed by CF's TSD destructor at thread exit.
leak:__CFTSDGetTable
# S6: libobjc's per-thread autorelease-pool page, created when a thread
# autoreleases with no pool in place; drained and freed by libobjc's TSD
# destructor at thread exit. Only the PAGE (the allocation inside
# autoreleaseNoPage); the objects autoreleased into it are not matched.
leak:AutoreleasePoolPage::autoreleaseNoPage
# S7: SkyLight (the window-server client under CoreGraphics/AppKit) receives a
# mach message into a malloc'd buffer inside its RunElsewhere dispatch source
# handler; the buffer is held by SkyLight's own source state.
leak:RunElsewhere::create_source
# S8: SkyLight's display-device cache: the plist (CFNumbers, CFData) it builds
# in update_devices_from_data_internal when the display configuration is first
# read or changes, held for the process.
leak:update_devices_from_data_internal
# S9: libFontRegistry (CoreText's font registry client): the process-wide
# registry it creates once (TGlobalFontRegistry::CreateRegistry under
# pthread_once) caches the server's rendezvous reply (dictionaries, CFURLs and
# strings decoded by NSXPC) for the life of the process. A CoreText
# create-then-release probe with no Rustty code reproduces these reports
# exactly, and Apple's `leaks` reports 0 for the same sequence.
leak:libFontRegistry.dylib
# S10: CoreText's font-descriptor cache (TDescriptorSource::CopyDescriptorUncached
# fills it); same probe and evidence as S9. A CTFont Rustty fails to release
# would be reported through TCFBase_NEW<CTFont>, which this does not match.
leak:TDescriptorSource::CopyDescriptorUncached
# S11: dyld's thread-local-variable storage block for a thread that is still
# alive when LSan scans at exit (here a test child that calls exit(3) from its
# test thread). dyld allocates it on the thread's first TLV access
# (_tlv_get_addr -> instantiateVariable) and frees it from its TSD destructor
# at thread exit; like S3-S6 it is reachable only through pthread TSD. Only the
# block itself; heap that thread-locals own is allocated elsewhere.
leak:dyld::ThreadLocalVariables::instantiateVariable
# S12: dyld's per-thread list of thread-local destructors, which std registers
# (_tlv_atexit -> addTermFunc) when a thread first touches a thread-local with a
# destructor; dyld runs and frees it at thread exit. Seen when a test child
# calls exit(3) from its test thread, so that thread is alive at the scan
# (rustty-cli's list_themes picker children). Same class as S11.
leak:dyld::ThreadLocalVariables::addTermFunc
# S13: CoreText's CGFont cache (TCGFontCache::CopyFont -> CGFontCreateWithPathAndName),
# filled the first time a font file's tables are read and held for the process,
# with the font's data provider and file reference beneath it. A CTFont Rustty
# fails to release is reported through TCFBase_NEW<CTFont>, which this does not
# match; Apple's `leaks` reports 0 for rustty-font-shaper's tests built natively.
leak:TCGFontCache::CopyFont
# S14: CoreAnimation's per-thread transaction (CA::Transaction::create, 8 KB),
# created on a thread's first layer change and kept in CA's thread-specific
# data until thread exit; Apple's `leaks` lists no CA::Transaction for the same
# test binary (rustty-apprt-c macos_window_host) built natively.
leak:CA::Transaction::create
# S15: CoreGraphics' ColorSync transform cache (CGColorSyncTransformCacheGetRetained,
# backed by libcache), filled the first time a colour conversion between two
# colour spaces runs and held for the process. Apple's `leaks` reports 0 for
# rustty-renderer's tests built natively.
leak:CGColorSyncTransformCacheGetRetained
# S16: CoreGraphics' cached vImage converter for a colour conversion
# (CGCMSConverterCreateCachedCGvImageConverter, "Cached" in its name), kept with
# the S15 transform; same evidence as S15.
leak:CGCMSConverterCreateCachedCGvImageConverter
# S17: the private dispatch queue a Metal command queue creates inside its own
# init (-[_MTLCommandQueue initWithDevice:descriptor:] ->
# _dispatch_lane_create_with_target). libdispatch disposes of it asynchronously
# once the command queue is released, so a process that exits right after
# freeing its renderer can still hold it at the scan (rustty-apprt-c's
# init-fault children; timing-dependent). Only allocations made INSIDE that
# init: a command queue Rustty fails to release is allocated by
# -[AGX...Device newCommandQueue]'s alloc, outside it, and stays reported.
# Apple's `leaks` on each init-fault child, run directly: 0 leaks (9 runs).
leak:-[_MTLCommandQueue initWithDevice:descriptor:]
